amentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Eric Sorensen (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term-plus incumbent Eric Sorensen seeks a third term in a northern Illinois district that leans Democratic by about three points. He enters the general election with a decisive financial advantage, having raised $2.2 million and holding $1.3 million on hand, while Republican nominee Dillan Vancil raised about $309,000 with modest reserves and no reported outside support.\n\nEvery major expert rating, from Cook Political Report to Sabato's Crystal Ball, places the race in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and prediction markets give Sorensen roughly a 94 percent chance of winning. No general-election polling is available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals, money, and ratings, all of which align. Local coverage has focused on Sorensen's constituent work and questions about Vancil's candidacy rather than a competitive race dynamic.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9829, "margin": 14.81}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9843, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9781, "margin": 15.0}]}